    "description": "A dropdown select with a label, optional description, a value row with a chevron, and an expandable options list. Ten variants cover five states crossed with two value types, plus boolean toggles for label, description and error text.\n\nPURPOSE: Lets a user pick one value from a list of options. The field shows the current value, or placeholder text when nothing has been chosen yet.\n\nBEHAVIOUR: State covers Default, Hover, Focus, Error and Disabled. The Open boolean controls whether the Options panel is shown below the field; Default, Error, Hover and Focus variants include an Options frame, while the Disabled variants do not. Focus adds a Stroke/Focus Ring with Border/Focus/Default. Error swaps the border to Border/Danger/Default and reveals the Error text in Text/Danger/Default. Disabled uses Background/Disabled/Default, Border/Disabled/default, Text/Disabled/Default and Icon/Disabled/On Disabled. Value Type switches the value text between a real selection and placeholder styling.\n\nCOMPOSITION: One nested instance, Chevron down, sits at the right of the Select frame. The Options frame holds five child layers for the open list. Code generators should look for an existing chevron or icon component and an existing option/list item component in the codebase before building new ones.\n\nUSAGE: Use for a single choice from a known, moderate list of options. For two or three options use radio buttons instead. For free text or a filterable list use a text input or combobox. Keep the label on and use Description for guidance that applies before the user picks, and Error for validation feedback after.\n\nCODE GENERATION NOTES: Map the Open boolean to the expanded state of the listbox, not to a separate component. Keep Label, Description, Value and Error as text slots. The Options frame in the design is static, so option rendering, keyboard navigation, type-ahead, selected and active option styling, and closing on outside click all need to be added i